  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 213 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nAs we go through this week we'll see western US ridging get\nsuppressed to the south as a trough moves through Canada and the\nnorthern tier of the US. This trough eventually settles into\nbroad troughing through central North America with ridging on\neither side, over the western US and the western Atlantic. Our\narea will be near the base of the trough for a few days in the \nmiddle to later part of this week before ridging becomes more \nexpansive across the southern half of the US this weekend. The \nimpacts for us locally will be increased rain chances as the \nbase of this trough nears our area.\n\nAt the surface, high pressure weakens as it moves east on\nTuesday. Our flow locally turns weakly southerly with at least\none more dry day in store for us. Temperatures again warm\nanother couple of degrees with another day of sun, putting us \nback into the mid to upper 80s. Meanwhile, as the initial trough\nmoves by to our north it sends a cold front southeast through\nthe Northern Plains. By Wednesday this front settles into a\nroughly west to east position across Nebraska and Iowa. A weak\ndisturbance tracking along it will trigger a round of \nthunderstorms to our west on Wednesday, with these storms moving\neast overnight into Thursday morning. Rain chances with this \nround are greatest in the northern portion of our forecast area,\ncloser to the front. However, this first round of storms may \nhelp to push the front southward into our area with the \npotential for additional storms to form near this front Thursday\nnight into Friday and then maybe again Friday night into \nSaturday. Confidence is greatest with the first round of storms.\nThe subsequent rounds depend in part on how the first round \nprogresses, resulting in greater uncertainty on where the \nsubsequent rounds form and track. Regardless this Wednesday \nnight through Saturday period is our best chance for rain this \nweek. The severe weather threat looks low, but with increasing \nmoisture available we could see locally heavy rainfall depending\non how the rounds of storms evolve.\n\nWhen it comes to temperatures we have strong confidence in a\nwarming trend continuing through Wednesday. NBM interquartile\nspread for high temperatures remains at a modest 2 to 3 degrees\neach day through Wednesday, reflecting a strong consensus among\nguidance through this warm up. However, that spread increases to\n7 degrees or more Thursday through the weekend, indicating lower\nconsensus during this period. This uncertainty is driven largely\nby the position of that front and any convection or related\ncloud cover along it limiting heating each day. On days where we\nsee more sun, we'll rise into the low 90s, but with more clouds\nor a more southward push of the front we likely stay in the mid\n80s.",
